# The two things on the field the player cannot walk through: a tree and a bush.
#
# **36 x 36 texels a frame, at `art::TEXELS_PER_UNIT` = 20**, which is 1.8 world
# units — `2 * PropKind::Tree.radius()`, the largest prop collider, to the texel.
# A `.crpix` declares one frame size for the whole file, and that is the whole
# reason this is a sheet of its own rather than more frames of an existing one:
# `terrain.crpix` is 40 and `actors.crpix` is 34, and neither can hold a 36.
#
# # Both props are drawn as a footprint, and that is what settles the layer order
#
# `art::Scene` puts the props above the ground and below everything that moves,
# which is the only order that works — a prop over the crowd hides the horde,
# and a prop under the ground is not drawn at all. The cost of that order is
# stated as a problem for tall scenery: a tree drawn in *elevation* has a canopy
# above its trunk, the player collides with the trunk, and the canopy is
# therefore painted under a wizard who is standing well below it. The picture
# says the wizard is in front of the treetop and the geometry says they are
# under it.
#
# So neither of these is drawn in elevation. This game is top-down — every
# actor's silhouette is its own collider to the texel, `art.rs` asserts it, and
# the ground is a lawn seen from above — and a prop drawn the same way has no
# "above" to get wrong. **The picture is exactly the ground the prop occupies**,
# so a wizard overlapping a canopy edge is a wizard standing on that patch of
# ground, which is the reading the crowd already has when two monsters overlap.
#
# The alternatives were both worse and both cost something measured.
# Splitting the sheet so a canopy could be drawn over the actors is a second run
# of one sheet in a frame, which is another draw call in `SceneStats::batches` —
# a number `art.rs`, `docs/plan/sample/03-horde.md` and the changelog all quote.
# Making the prop short enough that the overlap "does not arise" is this,
# without saying so.
#
# # 0.9 and 0.5 world units
#
# The tree is a little larger than `EnemyKind::Brute`'s 0.85, so the biggest
# thing on the field is scenery rather than a monster, and 0.9 is a whole 36
# texels at 20 a unit. The bush is exactly `game::PLAYER_RADIUS`: "can I get
# round this" is the only question a player asks about a prop, and the shape
# they judge it against is their own — 0.5 is a whole 20 texels.
#
# The bush is therefore 20 texels of art in a 36-texel frame, about a third of
# the fill, and the margin is transparent. That is the same trade
# `assets/actors.crpix` takes for the same reason and at about the same ratio:
# one sheet, one frame size, one batch, no emission order to get wrong. Props
# are also fixed in number by the seed rather than by the horde, so what the
# margin costs cannot grow with the field.
#
# # The palette sits between the grass and the crowd, and that is measured
#
# Two bounds, and neither is aesthetic. Below is `assets/terrain.crpix`: a prop
# inside the grass's own band is one the player walks into. Above is the horde:
# a static obstacle that out-shines a moving monster pulls the eye to the thing
# that cannot kill you, and there are tens of props against hundreds of
# monsters. So every frame here averages brighter than the brightest grass texel
# and dimmer than the dimmest monster's average, and
# `art::tests::the_props_sit_between_the_grass_and_the_crowd_in_luma` is what
# says so — against the other two baked sheets rather than against numbers
# written down here, so a repalette of either side moves the test instead of
# leaving this paragraph quietly wrong.
#
# The rim follows the monsters and not the player: dark outline, lighter fill.
# `assets/actors.crpix` gives the wizard the one bright edge on screen
# specifically so it can be found in a crowd, and scenery drawn with a bright
# edge is the thing that takes that away.
#
# # Nothing here animates and nothing here turns
#
# No clip, and `art::Scene` draws every prop at rotation zero. A prop that swayed
# would be a second clock in a game whose only animation is the walk cycle, and a
# rotated canopy is the same canopy — the quad is square and the shape is round,
# so the rotation would cost an instance field to hide nothing.

crpix: 36 36 2 7 1

palette:
  . c None
  k c #14151b   # the outline, the same one every actor in this game carries
  d c #26361f   # foliage, in shadow
  m c #384d2c   # foliage, and most of a canopy
  l c #4d6837   # foliage, lit — the light comes from the upper left
  t c #35281a   # bark, in shadow
  b c #4c3a25   # bark

